meta {
  name: Components (sensors)
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/metrics/components?start={{metrics_start}}&end={{metrics_end}}&resolution={{metrics_resolution}}&limit={{metrics_limit}}
  body: none
  auth: bearer
}

params:query {
  ~start: {{metrics_start}}
  ~end: {{metrics_end}}
  ~resolution: {{metrics_resolution}}
  ~limit: {{metrics_limit}}
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{jwt_token}}
}

docs {
  Hardware sensor history (sysinfo `Components` API). Each point is a
  `(timestamp, label)` pair; group client-side by `label` to draw a line
  per sensor. `temperature_c`, `max_c`, `critical_c` may be null when
  the OS lists a sensor without a current reading.

  Coverage:
  - Linux: lm-sensors / hwmon — typically Package id 0, per-core temps,
    NVMe Composite, GPU 0 / amdgpu, etc.
  - macOS: IOKit smart sensors — varies by Mac model
  - Windows: sysinfo's WMI bridge is limited; many machines (especially
    desktops without a temp-aware ACPI table) return zero sensors. The
    response is `points: []`, not an error — UI should treat the panel
    as "no data" rather than "broken".

  When a sensor reports `critical_c`, alerts should fire well below it —
  thermal throttling already kicks in before the manufacturer's number.
}
